ns, functools, heapq, itertools, json, math, operator, random, re, statistics, string. numpy/pandas are NOT available.\nPARTIAL FIDELITY: the name UNKNOWN is already injected into your program's namespace (use the bare name UNKNOWN \u2014 do not import or define it). render(state) MAY place UNKNOWN in any cell the program cannot model; the validator SKIPS every UNKNOWN cell, so an unmodelable region never fails validation. If a board region changes after every action regardless of which action (e.g. an energy/timer/progress bar), either model it EXACTLY or mark those cells UNKNOWN \u2014 do NOT assume it stays constant.\nOBJECTS: segment(grid) is already injected into your namespace (bare name \u2014 do not import or define it). It returns {nodes, adjacency_list}; each node has color, pixels, boundary, and children. PREFER expressing mechanics relative to objects rather than absolute cell indices: e.g. locate the avatar by its color with segment(grid) each step and move it (dr, dc), instead of hardcoding a row/col \u2014 absolute indices break on new levels where the same object sits elsewhere. Purely cellular games (no discrete movable objects) may still use direct grid logic.\n",
  "raw_llm_response": "```python\nimport copy\n\n# Global state object to track positions, sizes, and colors of all objects\n_world_state = None\n\ndef init_state(frame):\n    \"\"\"\n    Initialize the world model state from the starting frame (grid).\n    Parses the grid to identify distinct objects and their properties.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _world_state\n    \n    # Use segment to identify objects in the initial grid\n    seg = segment(frame)\n    \n    # Create a list of object descriptors\n    objects = []\n    for node_id, node in seg['nodes'].items():\n        obj = {\n            'id': node_id,\n            'color': node['color'],\n            'pixels': node['pixels'],\n            'bbox': node['boundary'], # [r_min, c_min, r_max, c_max]\n            'children': node.get('children', [])\n        }\n        objects.append(obj)\n    \n    _world_state = {\n        'objects': objects,\n        'grid_dims': (len(frame), len(frame[0])) if frame else (0, 0),\n        'last_grid': frame # Keep a reference to the last known grid for rendering if needed\n    }\n    \n    return copy.deepcopy(_world_state)\n\ndef step(state, action):\n    \"\"\"\n    Apply an action to the current state and return the new state and any events.\n    Since no specific mechanics are defined by the prompt other than initialization,\n    this function currently returns the state unchanged with no events.\n    In a full ARC solution, this would parse 'action' (e.g., 'ACTION1', 'ACTION4')\n    and modify _world_state or return a new grid based on game logic.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _world_state\n    \n    # For now, we just return the current state as no specific transition logic \n    # was provided in the prompt's \"Change to apply\" section beyond initialization.\n    # The prompt asks to initialize the state object.\n    \n    events = []\n    \n    # Return a deep copy of the state to avoid mutation issues\n    new_state = copy.deepcopy(state)\n    \n    return (new_state, events)\n\ndef render(state):\n    \"\"\"\n    Render the current state back into a grid.\n    Since we don't have specific rendering logic for dynamic changes yet,\n    we return the last known grid from the state or an empty grid if none exists.\n    In a full implementation, this would reconstruct the grid from _world_state objects.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _world_state\n    \n    # If we have a stored grid in the state (from init), use it.\n    # Otherwise, return an empty grid of the expected dimensions.\n    if 'last_grid' in state and state['last_grid'] is not None:\n        return copy.deepcopy(state['last_grid'])\n    \n    dims = state.get('grid_dims', (0, 0))\n    return [[0 for _ in range(dims[1])] for _ in range(dims[0])]\n\ndef is_win(state):\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if the current state is a winning state.\n    Default to False as no win condition is specified.\n    \"\"\"\n    return False\n\ndef legal_actions(state):\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a list of legal actions for the current state.\n    Since no specific game mechanics are defined, we return an empty list or a placeholder.\n    In a real ARC task, this would depend on the current grid configuration.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Placeholder: No specific actions defined in the prompt's change request.\n    return []\n```",
  "extracted_program_source": "import copy\n\n# Global state object to track positions, sizes, and colors of all objects\n_world_state = None\n\ndef init_state(frame):\n    \"\"\"\n    Initialize the world model state from the starting frame (grid).\n    Parses the grid to identify distinct objects and their properties.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _world_state\n    \n    # Use segment to identify objects in the initial grid\n    seg = segment(frame)\n    \n    # Create a list of object descriptors\n    objects = []\n    for node_id, node in seg['nodes'].items():\n        obj = {\n            'id': node_id,\n            'color': node['color'],\n            'pixels': node['pixels'],\n            'bbox': node['boundary'], # [r_min, c_min, r_max, c_max]\n            'children': node.get('children', [])\n        }\n        objects.append(obj)\n    \n    _world_state = {\n        'objects': objects,\n        'grid_dims': (len(frame), len(frame[0])) if frame else (0, 0),\n        'last_grid': frame # Keep a reference to the last known grid for rendering if needed\n    }\n    \n    return copy.deepcopy(_world_state)\n\ndef step(state, action):\n    \"\"\"\n    Apply an action to the current state and return the new state and any events.\n    Since no specific mechanics are defined by the prompt other than initialization,\n    this function currently returns the state unchanged with no events.\n    In a full ARC solution, this would parse 'action' (e.g., 'ACTION1', 'ACTION4')\n    and modify _world_state or return a new grid based on game logic.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _world_state\n    \n    # For now, we just return the current state as no specific transition logic \n    # was provided in the prompt's \"Change to apply\" section beyond initialization.\n    # The prompt asks to initialize the state object.\n    \n    events = []\n    \n    # Return a deep copy of the state to avoid mutation issues\n    new_state = copy.deepcopy(state)\n    \n    return (new_state, events)\n\ndef render(state):\n    \"\"\"\n    Render the current state back into a grid.\n    Since we don't have specific rendering logic for dynamic changes yet,\n    we return the last known grid from the state or an empty grid if none exists.\n    In a full implementation, this would reconstruct the grid from _world_state objects.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _world_state\n    \n    # If we have a stored grid in the state (from init), use it.\n    # Otherwise, return an empty grid of the expected dimensions.\n    if 'last_grid' in state and state['last_grid'] is not None:\n        return copy.deepcopy(state['last_grid'])\n    \n    dims = state.get('grid_dims', (0, 0))\n    return [[0 for _ in range(dims[1])] for _ in range(dims[0])]\n\ndef is_win(state):\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if the current state is a winning state.\n    Default to False as no win condition is specified.\n    \"\"\"\n    return False\n\ndef legal_actions(state):\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a list of legal actions for the current state.\n    Since no specific game mechanics are defined, we return an empty list or a placeholder.\n    In a real ARC task, this would depend on the current grid configuration.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Placeholder: No specific actions defined in the prompt's change request.\n    return []",
